                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Cracked this open on a rainy evening and suddenly I was 12 again with a Fighting Fantasy paperback and a pencil, except now the dice roll across the parchment page and the automap draws itself. Tin Man kept all of Livingstone's text and the original art intact (now in colour), and the unlimited bookmarks are a clever stand-in for jamming fingers between pages. Fair warning: Forest of Doom is one of the weaker books, since you need to hit very specific paths to find both hammer pieces and there's no turning back, so expect an arrow to the head and a restart or three before you win. A run is maybe an hour, the music loops a bit much, and this standalone edition has been folded into Fighting Fantasy Classics for cheaper, so grab it on sale or check that version first. Still a lovely, faithful bit of nostalgia for anyone who grew up on these books.",
